// One module reached two ways.
//
// `lib/shapes.frost` is named here, and named again by
// `lib/nested/perimeter.frost` as `../shapes.frost`. Those are two spellings of
// one file, and a file lands in a program once however many times it is named:
// twice would put every declaration in it beside a copy of itself, and a name
// that stands for two things stands for neither.
//
// Which is worth a program of its own because the second spelling only turns up
// once a project keeps its modules in directories. A tree that is all one
// directory never writes a `..` and never asks the question.

import "lib/shapes.frost"
import "lib/nested/perimeter.frost"
import "io.frost"

main :: fn() -> i64 {
    r := Rect { width = 3, height = 4 }
    print("{}\n{}\n", area(r), perimeter(r))
    0
}
